{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-07-22T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some structures weren't meant to stand out—but time had other ideas. At sunrise, the Santa Catalina Arch glows over the streets of Antigua, Guatemala, its yellow façade catching the light just right as cobblestones stretch ahead and Agua Volcano rises in the distance. The arch was built in the 17th century to solve a practical problem: cloistered nuns needed to cross between convent buildings without being seen from the street. Privacy, not postcards, created one of Central America's most photographed landmarks. Irony holds up well over time.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SantaCatalina_EN-CA0068780262_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SantaCatalina_EN-CA0068780262_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Hidden in plain arch","Title":"Santa Catalina Arch, Antigua, Guatemala","Copyright":"© Filippo Maria Bianchi/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/14.559567,-90.73426/3?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=14.559567,-90.73426;S9;Santa Catalina Arch, Antigua, Sacatepéquez, Guatemala&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260721_SantaCatalina\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Santa+Catalina+Arch+Guatemala&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260721_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260721_SantaCatalina"},"Ssd":"20260721","Name":"SantaCatalina","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"0068780262","FullDateString":"Jul 21, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"From the moon's cratered surface, Earth appears as a thin, glowing crescent against the darkness—a rare view that reverses our usual perspective. This stark landscape, shaped by billions of years of impacts, preserves a record of the early solar system. July 20 marks International Moon Day, commemorating the 1969 Apollo 11 landing, when humans first set foot on the lunar surface. Established by the United Nations in 2021, the day highlights past achievements and ongoing efforts to explore the moon.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.Artemis_EN-CA9898142366_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.Artemis_EN-CA9898142366_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"The lunar perspective","Title":"Moon and Earth captured by the Artemis II crew","Copyright":"© NASA","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"The moon rotates at the same rate it orbits Earth, so we always see the same side from our planet.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260720_Artemis\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=International+Moon+Day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260720_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260720_Artemis"},"Ssd":"20260720","Name":"Artemis","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"9898142366","FullDateString":"Jul 20, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Perched on slender reeds, a group of barn swallows gathers in a moment of stillness that contrasts with their usual speed. Known for swift, acrobatic flight and deeply forked tails, these birds are built for life on the wing. They catch insects midair and travel vast distances between continents. Subtle variations in colour and markings suggest that more than one subspecies may be present, reflecting the barn swallow's extensive global range.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.HirundoRustica_EN-CA9613012856_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.HirundoRustica_EN-CA9613012856_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Wings at rest","Title":"Barn swallows of different subspecies resting together","Copyright":"© Oscar Dominguez/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"Barn swallows can travel thousands of kilometres between their breeding and wintering grounds each year.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260719_HirundoRustica\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Barn+swallow&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260719_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260719_HirundoRustica"},"Ssd":"20260719","Name":"HirundoRustica","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"9613012856","FullDateString":"Jul 19, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Reflected in still water, the Rakotzbrücke—commonly known as the Devil's Bridge—forms a near-perfect stone circle in Saxony, Germany. Commissioned in 1860, the narrow basalt structure was carefully engineered so that its arch and reflection align to form a seamless ring when the lake is still. Made from basalt, a volcanic rock, it reflects precise design rather than practical function, its striking geometry intended purely for visual effect. A popular photo subject, it has also appeared in films, including 'The Matrix Resurrections' (2021). Today, the fragile bridge can only be admired from the shore, as crossing is prohibited to preserve it.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.DevilsBridge_EN-CA9488720591_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.DevilsBridge_EN-CA9488720591_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Going full circle","Title":"Devil's Bridge in Rhododendron Park Kromlau, Saxony, Germany","Copyright":"© Mike Mareen/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/51.53661,14.640453/4?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=51.53661,14.640453;S9;Devil's Bridge, Kromlau, Saxony, Germany&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260718_DevilsBridge\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Devils+Bridge+Kromlau&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260718_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260718_DevilsBridge"},"Ssd":"20260718","Name":"DevilsBridge","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"9488720591","FullDateString":"Jul 18, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Picture a farmer watching dark clouds build over the Saskatchewan plains. The crops have already been harvested, but the weather still matters. Where does the grain go next? Often, into grain elevators and storage bins beside fields and railway lines, where it stays protected from rain, moisture and the powerful prairie storms that can arrive with little warning. These storage structures have supported Saskatchewan agriculture for generations, helping to preserve grain quality until it is ready for transport or sale. This is one reason the province has earned the nickname 'Canada's breadbasket.'","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GrainSilosSK_EN-CA7421778186_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GrainSilosSK_EN-CA7421778186_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"The breadbasket of Canada","Title":"A prairie storm gathers over grain silos in Saskatchewan","Copyright":"© mysticenergy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"In 2025, Saskatchewan produced 92 per cent of Canada's chickpeas.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260717_GrainSilosSK\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Saskatchewan&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260717_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260717_GrainSilosSK"},"Ssd":"20260717","Name":"GrainSilosSK","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"7421778186","FullDateString":"Jul 17, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some places are built for a single purpose; others keep reinventing themselves. Navy Pier in Chicago, Illinois, United States, falls into the second category. The structure stretches more than 915 metres into the lake and was designed as part of Edward H. Bennett's and Daniel Burnham's 1909 Plan of Chicago. Opened in 1916, the pier was built for shipping, recreation and passenger travel on Lake Michigan. It was renamed Navy Pier in honour of World War I naval veterans.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_EN-CA7051482723_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_EN-CA7051482723_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"A waterfront chameleon","Title":"Navy Pier, Chicago, Illinois, United States","Copyright":"© Christopher.F Photography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/41.892227,-87.605187/3?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=41.892227,-87.605187;S9;Navy Pier, Chicago, Illinois, United States&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Navy+Pier+Chicago&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260716_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier"},"Ssd":"20260716","Name":"NavyPier","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"7051482723","FullDateString":"Jul 16, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"If mountains could keep a record of the past, the John Muir Wilderness near Bishop, California, would have plenty to share. Its valleys, lakes and granite formations reveal a history shaped over millions of years. Established in 1964, this vast protected area spans more than 2,630 square kilometres of the Sierra Nevada and is named after naturalist John Muir. Today, parts of the Pacific Crest Trail and John Muir Trail pass through the area, drawing hikers into one of the largest high-elevation wilderness regions in the contiguous United States.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MarieLake_EN-CA6168691994_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MarieLake_EN-CA6168691994_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"The reward after the climb","Title":"Marie Lake, John Muir Wilderness near Bishop, California, United States","Copyright":"© Steve Dunleavy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/36.97615,-118.812278/4?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=36.97615,-118.812278;S9;John Muir Wilderness, California, USA&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260715_MarieLake\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=John+Muir+Wilderness&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260715_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260715_MarieLake"},"Ssd":"20260715","Name":"MarieLake","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"6168691994","FullDateString":"Jul 15, 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Off Anacapa Island, California in the United States, the ocean grows its own forests. Within Channel Islands National Park—also known as the 'Galápagos of North America'—giant kelp stretches beyond 30 metres in length, forming buoyant ribbons lifted by air-filled bladders and anchored below by root-like holdfasts.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_EN-CA9531487501_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_EN-CA9531487501_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"A balance in motion","Title":"A kelp forest off Anacapa Island, Channel Islands National Park, California, United States","Copyright":"© Ian Shive/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/34.004342,-119.391995/3?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=34.004342,-119.391995;S9;Anacapa Island, California, United States&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=World+Nature+Conservation+Day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260728_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp"},"Ssd":"20260728","Name":"ChannelKelp","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"9531487501","FullDateString":"Jul 28, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Step inside the Chicago Cultural Centre, and your eyes are instantly drawn upward to a glowing canopy of colour—the Tiffany Dome. Suspended above Preston Bradley Hall, this masterpiece spans approximately 11 metres in diameter and is composed of roughly 30,000 pieces of Favrile glass, each shaped like a fish scale. Sunlight filters through its translucent surface, shifting blues and golds across its intricate pattern.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ChicagoTiffany_EN-CA9232454244_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ChicagoTiffany_EN-CA9232454244_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":false},"Headline":"Chicago, look up!","Title":"Tiffany Dome, Chicago Cultural Centre, Illinois, United States","Copyright":"© Felix Lipov/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/41.88388,-87.62498/3?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=41.88388,-87.62498;S9;Tiffany Dome, Chicago Cultural Centre, Chicago, USA&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260727_ChicagoTiffany\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Tiffany+Dome+Chicago+Cultural+Center&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260727_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260727_ChicagoTiffany"},"Ssd":"20260727","Name":"ChicagoTiffany","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"9232454244","FullDateString":"Jul 27, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"At sunrise in Honduras' Pig Keys—small, low-lying coastal islets tucked within mangrove-rich wetlands—a red mangrove lifts its tangled roots above glassy water. This striking scene captures what July 26 celebrates: the International Day for the Conservation of the Mangrove Ecosystem, created to spotlight these vital coastal forests.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_EN-CA8849634199_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_EN-CA8849634199_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Between land and sea, life begins","Title":"Sunrise on a red mangrove in the Pig Keys, Honduras","Copyright":"© Mac Stone/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/0.822356,-86.5979/4?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=0.822356,-86.5979;S9;Honduras&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=International+Day+for+Conservation+of+the+Mangrove+Ecosystem&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260726_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise"},"Ssd":"20260726","Name":"RedMangroveSunrise","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"8849634199","FullDateString":"Jul 26, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In Spain's far northwest, Galicia meets the Atlantic with force—its long coastline carved by waves into cliffs, inlets and wave-cut platforms. Rain-fed hills and dense forests spill toward the sea, creating a landscape that feels closer to Ireland than southern Spain, with shifting skies and a distinctly Atlantic mood.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GaliciaBeach_EN-CA4896799144_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GaliciaBeach_EN-CA4896799144_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Into Galicia's hidden arches","Title":"Praia das Catedrais, Galicia, Spain","Copyright":"© Tui De Roy/Amazing Aerial Agency/Abobe Stock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/43.554231,-7.157587/4?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=43.554231,-7.157587;S9;Praia das Catedrais, Galicia, Spain&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260725_GaliciaBeach\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Galicia+Spain&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260725_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260725_GaliciaBeach"},"Ssd":"20260725","Name":"GaliciaBeach","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"4896799144","FullDateString":"Jul 25, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Today is National Cousins Day—a perfect excuse to reconnect with the people who know your family stories best and probably helped create a few of them. Fittingly, Canadians mark the day alongside their neighbour and, you could say, cousin to the south: the United States. Whether you grew up at the cottage, camping, skating or gathering for a long weekend barbecue, cousins often share some of life's best memories.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GalapagosFlamingos_EN-CA4325829188_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GalapagosFlamingos_EN-CA4325829188_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Family, in full colour","Title":"Flock of American flamingos, Isabela Island, Galápagos Islands, Ecuador","Copyright":"© Tui De Roy/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"Vampire Weekend, the indie rock band, released their single 'Cousins' in 2009.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260724_GalapagosFlamingos\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=National+Cousins+Day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260724_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260724_GalapagosFlamingos"},"Ssd":"20260724","Name":"GalapagosFlamingos","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"4325829188","FullDateString":"Jul 24, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some flowers quietly fill a garden. Dahlias arrive as if they have been cast in the lead role. Native to Mexico, these members of the daisy family come in more than 57,000 cultivated varieties, thanks in part to their unusual genetics. Dahlias are octoploids, meaning they have eight sets of chromosomes, which helps create blooms that range from tiny buttons to 36 centimetres across.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.PinkDahlia_EN-CA0488598633_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.PinkDahlia_EN-CA0488598633_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"A symphony of petals","Title":"Pink dahlia flower","Copyright":"© Harald Biebel/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"One Aztec name for the dahlia, often translated as 'water pipe flower,' refers to the plant's long, hollow stems, which were used to transport water.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260723_PinkDahlia\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Dahlia+genus+plant&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260723_0400\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260723_PinkDahlia"},"Ssd":"20260723","Name":"PinkDahlia","Market":"EN-CA","Hash":"0488598633","FullDateString":"Jul 23, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Imagine a city built by animals, glowing in the sunlight and bustling with life beneath the waves. That is a coral reef. During Coral Reef Awareness Week, we celebrate these remarkable ecosystems and the countless species that depend on them.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CoralAwareness_EN-CA0311926536_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CoralAwareness_EN-CA0311926536_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Small footprint, big impact","Title":"Coral reef and beach in Raja Ampat, Indonesia","Copyright":"© SergeUWPhoto/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/1.050448,131.267654/4?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-CA&od=2&shading=flat&pp=1.050448,131.267654;S9;Raja Ampat, Southwest Papua, Indonesia&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260722_CoralAwareness\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Coral+Reef+Awareness+Week&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260722_0400\"+mgzv3configlis